        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "fb88eb89-6961-58e0-a221-266e03a3ec82",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Products: Korean Jindo, Taigan, Nureongi\n- Organizations: United Kennel Club, Fédération Cynologique Internationale, national kennel clubs\n- Places: Kyrgyzstan, Korea\n- Concepts: dog breed recognition, sighthounds, spitz dogs\n\nFacts:\n- The Korean Jindo breed became recognized by the United Kennel Club on January 1, 1998.\n- The Korean Jindo breed became recognized by the Fédération Cynologique Internationale in 2005.\n- The Taigan is a sighthound breed from Kyrgyzstan.\n- The Taigan is also known as Kyrgyzdyn Taighany and as Kyrgyzskaya Borzaya Taigan in Russian.\n- The Taigan is not yet recognized by the Fédération Cynologique Internationale, but it is recognized by a number of kennel clubs at the national level.\n- Nureongi, like the Korean Jindo and other native Korean dogs, is a medium-sized spitz.\n- Nureongi has greater musculature and distinctive coat patterns.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "16020ddf-a14b-5941-ae4c-c0b4e2057fe3",
        "text": "Page: Korean Jindo. Sentence: The Jindo breed became recognized by the United Kennel Club on January 1, 1998 and by the Fédération Cynologique Internationale in 2005.\nPage: Taigan. Sentence: The Taigan (Kyrgyz: Тайган ) also known as Kyrgyzdyn Taighany (Kyrgyzskaya Borzaya Taigan in Russian) is a sighthound breed from Kyrgyzstan, which is not yet recognized by the FCI, but is recognized by a number of kennel clubs on national level.\nPage: Nureongi. Sentence: Like native Korean dogs such as the Korean Jindo, Korean Yellow spitzes are medium-sized spitz, but with greater musculature and distinctive coat patterns.",
